.TH LOGIN 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
login \- begin a session on the system
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B login
.RI [ username ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B login
authenticates a user on the terminal connected to its standard input
and starts that user's login shell.
It is normally run by
.B getty
or
.B telnetd
as the superuser, and must run as the superuser to be able to change
identity.
.PP
With no
.I username
operand,
.B login
prints the contents of
.I /etc/issue
verbatim, followed by the line
.RI "Substrate (" tty )
where
.I tty
is the terminal name with any leading
.I /dev/
removed.
It then prompts
.B login:
for a user name.
If
.I username
is given (as
.B getty
does), the banner and the first user-name prompt are skipped.
An operand beginning with
.B \-
is ignored, as are all operands after the first.
.PP
The password is then read from the
.B Password:
prompt with terminal echo turned off.
The password prompt is shown even when the user does not exist.
.PP
The password is checked against the second field of the user's
entry in
.IR /etc/shadow .
If the user has no shadow entry, the password field of
.I /etc/passwd
is used instead, unless it begins with
.BR x .
The stored value is interpreted as follows:
.TP
empty
No password is required.
.TP
begins with \fB*\fR or \fB!\fR
The account is locked; no password is accepted.
.TP
anything else
A
.BR crypt (3)
hash.
The typed password is hashed with the stored value as the setting
and the results are compared.
Plaintext passwords are not accepted.
.PP
On failure,
.B Login incorrect
is printed and, after a 2-second delay, the user name is prompted for
again.
After 3 failed attempts
.B login
exits.
An empty user name counts as a failed attempt.
End-of-file (typing the EOF character) at either prompt does not
count as an attempt; the user-name prompt is shown again.
.PP
On success
.B login
does the following, in order:
.IP \(bu 2
Writes a
.B USER_PROCESS
record for the terminal to
.I /var/run/utmp
and appends it to
.IR /var/log/wtmp .
The remote host field is taken from the
.B REMOTEHOST
environment variable, if set.
.IP \(bu
Changes the owner of the terminal to the user and its group to
.B tty
(or the user's login group if there is no
.B tty
group), with mode 0620.
.IP \(bu
Sets the group ID, initializes the supplementary groups from
.IR /etc/group ,
and sets the user ID.
.IP \(bu
Removes
.BR IFS ,
.BR ENV ,
.BR BASH_ENV ,
.BR CDPATH ,
.BR LD_PRELOAD ,
.BR LD_LIBRARY_PATH ,
.BR PS1 ,
.BR PS2 ,
.BR PWD ,
and
.B OLDPWD
from the environment and sets the variables listed under
ENVIRONMENT.
Other inherited variables are kept.
.IP \(bu
Changes to the home directory, or to
.I /
if that fails.
.IP \(bu
Resets the disposition of signals 1 to 31 to the default, calls
.BR setsid (2),
and executes the login shell from the password entry
.RI ( /bin/sh
if the field is empty) with
.I argv[0]
set to
.B \-
followed by the shell's base name, which requests a login shell.
If that fails it tries the shell with a
.B \-l
argument, then with no arguments.
.PP
.B login
does not write the logout record itself.
For sessions started by
.BR getty ,
.B init
writes the
.B DEAD_PROCESS
record when it reaps the session.
.SH EXIT STATUS
On success
.B login
does not return; it is replaced by the user's shell.
.TP
.B 1
Three login attempts failed.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TERM
Preserved for the shell; set to
.B linux
if not set.
.TP
.B REMOTEHOST
If set, recorded as the remote host in the
.I utmp
and
.I wtmp
records.
.PP
The following are set for the shell:
.TP
.BR HOME ", " SHELL
From the password entry.
.TP
.BR USER ", " LOGNAME
The user name.
.TP
.B PATH
.I /usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin:/usr/dt/bin
for the superuser,
.I /usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/dt/bin
for everyone else.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/issue
Banner shown before the first prompt.
.TP
.I /etc/passwd
User database.
.TP
.I /etc/shadow
Password hashes.
.TP
.I /etc/group
Supplementary groups and the
.B tty
group.
.TP
.I /var/run/utmp
Current login records.
.TP
.I /var/log/wtmp
Login history.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR id (1),
.BR last (1),
.BR newgrp (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR setsid (2),
.BR setuid (2),
.BR setgid (2),
.BR getpwent_r (3)
.SH BUGS
User names longer than 63 characters are silently truncated, and the
rest of the line is then read as the password.
.PP
If the shell cannot be executed,
.B login
reports the error and returns to the user-name prompt, but it is by
then already running as the authenticated user.
